  "query_preview": "(Today through Saturday)...\nIssued at 304 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday night...\n\nQuiet weather conditions are expected to continue as surface high \npressure remains in control. Current satellite and surface \nobservations depict surface high pressure centered near southern IL \nwith clear skies across the forecast area. A very weak pressure \ngradient from the surface high is contributing to very light or calm \nwinds. This combined with clear skies has allowed for patchy fog to \ndevelop in climatologically favored locations. Any fog that develops \nshould quickly mix out after sunrise. \n\nA low pressure system moving across southern Canada is expected to \npush a weak cold front through the region tonight. Meanwhile, high \npressure remaining south of central Indiana will continue to greatly \nlimit gulf moisture return, allowing for dry conditions to persist. \nBreezy conditions are likely ahead of the boundary due to a moderate \npressure gradient. Winds should quickly taper off during the evening \nhours once the pressure gradient begins to relax and the PBL \ndecouples. Winds becoming light overnight combined with mostly clear \nskies will support additional fog development into Monday morning.  \n\nUpper ridging and surface high pressure building across the region \nearly in the work week will continue to promote quiet weather.\nEnsemble guidance depicts a shortwave pushing another weak cold \nfront into central Indiana on Tuesday. However, no precipitation is \nexpected due to gulf moisture remaining cutoff. It remains uncertain \nif the front pushes through the entire forecast area as the front \neventually stalls Tuesday night.\n\nReturn flow will moderate temperatures over the next few days. Look \nfor highs in the upper 50s to low 60s through Monday with highs then \nwarming well into the 60s on Tuesday. \n\nWednesday through the end of the week... \n\nA shortwave moving in from the northwest late Wednesday into \nThursday will promote increasing rain chances. Latest ensemble \nguidance depicts surface high pressure breaking down across the \nSouth. This should allow for sufficient gulf moisture return ahead \nof a developing low pressure system associated with the shortwave. \nIncreasing dynamics combined with sufficient forcing supports the \npotential for scattered showers. \n\nThe parent trough is expected to continue deepening across the \neastern CONUS through late week with strong cold air advection \nacross central Indiana. Model guidance also depicts surface high \npressure building in Friday and Saturday. Look for much cooler \ntemperatures late this week and quiet weather. Highs are only \nexpected to reach the low to mid 50s by Thursday while highs \nstruggle to get out of the 40s by Friday. Lows in the upper 20s to \nlow 30s return Thursday and Friday night.",
